@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Simple object diagram — runtime snapshot of a bank customer, account, and a single transaction.
'   usecase:      Teach instance specification (name:Class), slots with concrete values, and links as instance associations.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       Banking
' ============================================================================
title Example Object Diagram - Simple Bank Snapshot

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' An OBJECT DIAGRAM shows a snapshot of instantiated objects at a point in
' time.  Each box is an Instance Specification written as "name:ClassName".
' The name portion is optional (anonymous: ":Account"); the class portion is
' optional too but usually present.  In PlantUML the `object` keyword renders
' the instance name underlined, which is the UML convention for instances.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

' Instance: john — a concrete Customer with slot values (attribute = value).
object "john:Customer" as john {
  ' Slots hold concrete runtime values, not type declarations.
  name = "John Smith"
  customerId = 42
  email = "john@example.com"
}

' Instance: acct1 — a checking Account owned by john.
object "acct1:Account" as acct1 {
  accountId = 1001
  balance = 1500.00
  type = "CHECKING"
}

' Instance: tx1 — one Transaction applied to the account.
object "tx1:Transaction" as tx1 {
  txnId = 77001
  amount = 200.00
  timestamp = "2026-07-09T10:15:00Z"
}

' LINKS are instances of associations — they connect specific objects, not
' classes.  A link can carry a role / association-name label.
john "1" --> "owns *" acct1
acct1 "1" --> "records *" tx1 : posted

note right of john
  <b>Reading guide</b>
  Each box = one runtime object.
  Underlined name = instance (not type).
  Field lines = slots (concrete values).
  Arrows = links (instance associations).
end note

@enduml
